/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The fuel injector (nozzle holder) is the body that carries the nozzle into the cylinder head.
   - The nozzle is a hardened needle on a conical seat with one or more holes below it.
   - Below the seat is a small cavity, the sac.
   - A nozzle is tested on a pop tester: a hand pump, a pressure gauge and a holder.
   - A sharp chatter during the pop shows the needle is free and seating.
   - A jet leaving a small hole is unstable: air drag and turbulence tear it into ligaments, then drops.
   - ṁ is the fuel mass flow (kg/s); Cd is the discharge coefficient (about 0.7); A is the total hole area (m²); ρ is the fuel density (about 830 kg/m³); Δp is injection minus cylinder pressure (Pa).
   - Penetration S is the distance the spray tip has reached from the nozzle at time t after the start of injection.
   - S = 2.95 (Δp ÷ ρa)0.25 √(d t) S in m; ρa is the cylinder air density (kg/m³); d is the hole diameter (m); t in s.
*/
