/* book exam traps: Putting the diameter straight into A = π d² and forgetting the divide by 4. A 0.25 mm hole gives 4.909×10⁻⁸ m², not 1.96×10⁻⁷ m². | Mixing opening pressure with injection pressure. Opening pressure is set by the nozzle spring (the needle lift point, around 100–250 bar). Injection pressure is the pump or rail pressure, which is much higher. | Saying more holes always means a better spray. More holes at the same total area make each hole smaller: atomisation improves, but each jet is weaker, penetrates less, and jets may merge in a small chamber. | Confusing pintle and throttle nozzles. Both have a pin in the hole; only the throttle nozzle's pin is shaped to throttle the flow at low needle lift. | Forgetting sac volume. The fuel trapped below the needle seat after closing burns late and adds hydrocarbons and smoke, so a worn sac is a real fault, not a detail. */
