/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Petrol engines use a throttle plate.
   - Without a governor, a sudden load drop leaves surplus fuel in the cycle.
   - The rack is the control rod of the injection pump (see Jerk pump, distributor pump and unit injector).
   - Signal path: spring tension sets target speed.
   - Vehicle diesels mostly use a two-speed (idle and maximum-speed) governor: it holds a steady idle and stops over-speeding, while the driver's pedal sets the fuel in between.
   - Two flyballs (flyweights) are carried on arms on a spindle geared to the engine, usually from the pump camshaft.
   - At steady speed, the outward force of the balls balances the spring force at one radius.
   - F is the centrifugal force on one ball (N); m is the ball mass (kg); ω is the spindle angular speed (rad/s); r is the radius of the ball's circular path about the spindle axis (m).
   - Because F depends on ω², doubling speed gives four times the force.
   - A pneumatic governor senses speed through air flow, not flyballs.
*/
